              <span>How does a mortgage offset account work in Australia?</span>
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#00F5D4] shrink-0 transition-transform duration-200" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"></path>
              </svg>
            </button>
            <div class="hidden p-4 sm:p-5 pt-0 text-sm text-slate-300 border-t border-[#1E294B]/50 leading-relaxed">
              A 100% offset account is a transaction account linked to your home loan. The balance in this account is offset against your outstanding loan principal when calculating interest. For example, if you owe $400,000 and have $50,000 in your offset account, you only pay interest on $350,000. The money remains accessible at any time, giving you flexibility that a direct lump-sum repayment does not.
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="rounded-xl bg-[#0A0F1D] border border-[#1E294B] overflow-hidden">
            <button 
              type="button"
              onclick="this.nextElementSibling.classList.toggle('hidden'); this.querySelector('svg').classList.toggle('rotate-180');" 
              class="w-full p-4 sm:p-5 text-left font-semibold text-white flex items-center justify-between gap-4 hover:bg-slate-900/50 transition-colors focus:outline-none"
            >
              <span>What is the difference between an offset account and a redraw facility?</span>
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#00F5D4] shrink-0 transition-transform duration-200" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"></path>
              </svg>
            </button>
            <div class="hidden p-4 sm:p-5 pt-0 text-sm text-slate-300 border-t border-[#1E294B]/50 leading-relaxed">
              An offset account is a separate transaction account whose balance reduces the interest charged on your home loan; the funds remain yours and are instantly accessible. A redraw facility lets you access extra repayments you've already made on the loan itself, but availability and processing times vary by lender. The key distinction is that offset funds never actually reduce your loan balance — they just reduce the interest calculation — while redraw involves money that has already been applied to the principal.
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Methodology Section -->
      <section class="glass-panel p-6 sm:p-8 rounded-2xl space-y-4">
        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-3 py-1 rounded-full bg-[#7B61FF]/10 text-[#7B61FF] text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider border border-[#7B61FF]/20">
          <span>Calculation Logic</span>
        </div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white tracking-tight">Methodology</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-sm sm:text-base text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          <p>
            This calculator models the interest-saving effect of deploying a lump sum against an Australian home loan. For a direct extra repayment or redraw scenario, it deducts the lump sum from the outstanding principal and re-amortises the reduced balance over the remaining loan term using the standard annuity formula at the current interest rate. For an offset account scenario, the lump sum is treated as a persistent offset balance that reduces the principal on which daily interest is calculated, while the monthly repayment amount remains unchanged — the savings manifest as a shorter loan term and reduced total interest.
          </p>
          <p>
            Australian home loans typically calculate interest daily on the outstanding balance, so the effective monthly interest cost is based on the daily rate multiplied by the number of days in each month. This calculator uses a monthly approximation (annual rate ÷ 12) which closely tracks the actual daily calculation over the full loan term. The model assumes a fixed interest rate; for variable-rate loans, results represent the impact at the current rate and will change if the rate adjusts.
          </p>
          <p>
            Offset accounts may come with higher monthly fees or a slightly higher interest rate than basic variable loans. This calculator does not automatically factor in the annual offset account fee — compare the interest savings against any additional fees your lender charges for the offset feature to determine net benefit.
          </p>
          <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-[#0A0F1D] border border-[#1E294B] text-xs text-slate-400 space-y-2">
            <p><strong>Official Reference:</strong> <a href="https://moneysmart.gov.au/home-loans/mortgage-calculator" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-[#00F5D4] hover:underline font-medium">ASIC Moneysmart — Mortgage Calculator →</a></p>
            <p class="italic text-slate-500">Calculations are estimates. Consult an ASIC-licensed financial adviser or mortgage broker for personalised guidance on offset accounts and home loan strategies.</p>
          </div>
